Approach: store each number's index in a dictionary; for each element, check whether its complement (target − num) was already seen. Using `long` for the complement avoids overflow at int extremes.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution {
    public int[] TwoSum(int[] nums, int target) {
        var seen = new Dictionary<int, int>();
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++) {
            long complement = (long)target - nums[i];
            if (complement >= int.MinValue && complement <= int.MaxValue
                && seen.TryGetValue((int)complement, out int j))
                return new[] { j, i };
            seen[nums[i]] = i;
        }
        return Array.Empty<int>();
    }
}
```

Time: O(n) | Space: O(n)

I'm assuming you're asking about the **"Leaders in an Array"** problem: find every element that is greater than or equal to all elements to its right.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static List<int> FindLeaders(int[] arr)
    {
        var leaders = new List<int>();
        if (arr == null || arr.Length == 0) return leaders;

        int max = int.MinValue;
        for (int i = arr.Length - 1; i >= 0; i--)
        {
            if (arr[i] >= max)
            {
                leaders.Add(arr[i]);
                max = arr[i];
            }
        }
        leaders.Reverse();
        return leaders;
    }
}
```

Time: O(n). Space: O(1) extra, O(k) for the result list.

